            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for flexible, then the French for to consider."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: le but",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "le but — \"the aim, the goal\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Mon but, c'est de trouver un travail à l'étranger. My aim is to find a job abroad."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In football, un but is a goal. The final t is often said."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "but comes from Old French but \"end, aim\" (EtymDB), linked to butte \"a mound used as a target\"; it is usually traced to a Frankish word for a stump or a log."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
